            <ArticleTitle>Conservative correction of leg-length discrepancies of 10mm or less for the relief of chronic low back pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2075-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To study whether conservative correction in a leg-length discrepancy (LLD) of 10mm or less in patients with chronic low back pain (CLBP) can relieve pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Randomized, controlled intervention study, with a mean follow-up duration of 10 weeks.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Physical therapy clinic of the national health services.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty-three patients with CLBP were screened for an LLD of 10mm or less, which was measured with ultrasound. Patients were randomly divided into intervention and control groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">In 22 patients, LLD was corrected by applying individually fitted shoe inserts. In 11 patients, LLD was not corrected.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Chronic pain intensity (visual analog scale) and disability score (Roland-Morris Disability Questionnaire).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Shoe inserts significantly reduced both pain intensity (P&lt;.001) and disability (P&lt;.05). A moderate positive correlation was found between LLD and the degree of pain relief after wearing shoe inserts (r=.47).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Shoe inserts appear to reduce CLBP and functional disability in patients with LLDs of 10mm or less. Shoe inserts are simple, noninvasive, and inexpensive therapeutic means that can be added to the treatment of CLBP.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
